{
  "_meta": {
    "phase": 3,
    "purpose": "Curated composite-dish templates for backend/src/services/food/decompose.js (Strategy C2). Each entry structures a dish that has NO reliable single measured DB row into its typical components, so a query like 'papdi chaat' is priced as papdi + potato + chickpeas + curd + chutneys + sev instead of matching whatever single row happens to contain both tokens (the failure mode documented in docs/FOOD_ESTIMATION_AUDIT_2026-08-28.md as the papdi-chaat bug).",
    "component_name_convention": "Component `name` strings are deliberately generic, common-DB terms (rice, curd, boiled potato, tamarind chutney, papdi, sev, ...) so they resolve against the SAME unified_food_db.json every other tier searches -- never a name coined only for this file.",
    "fraction_source": "Hand-curated from standard published recipes (household-measure proportions), cross-checked against indb_dishes.json where a close pre-composited analogue exists. NOT derived from a single authoritative recipe database -- treat as a reasonable typical proportion, not a lab measurement. `typical_fraction` values are of EDIBLE PORTION MASS and are expected to sum to ~1.0 per dish (decompose.js re-normalizes if they don't, before mass reconciliation against the requested total).",
    "coverage": "54 dishes across chaat/street-food, North Indian combos, South Indian combos, and pav-based snacks -- the classes that dominated the worst-25 identity failures in the Phase-0 benchmark. NOT the full ~200-dish aspiration from the original architecture note; this is a first, benchmark-gated batch. Expand by adding entries here, never by hand-coding a dish in decompose.js.",
    "schema": "{ <dish_key>: { aliases: string[], cuisine: string, typical_serving_g: number, components: [{ name: string, typical_fraction: number, prep_state: string }] } }"
  },

